  "interpretation": {
    "primary": "Even with nuisance known exactly and the velocity-error standard deviation reduced by a finite factor of ten, the lowest-bin information point estimate is 0.6900 and its 95 percent geometry-bootstrap lower bound is 0.6836, below the frozen 0.8 requirement. Velocity-only information is therefore insufficient for the requested stable parent posterior under every preregistered finite scenario.",
    "nuisance_contribution": "At the original velocity-error scale, making the nuisance known increases lowest-bin information by only 0.0281. Nuisance marginalization is not the dominant information loss in this audit.",
    "measurement_error_contribution": "Reducing the velocity-error standard deviation to 0.3 and 0.1 raises lowest-bin information by 0.2167 and 0.3578 relative to known-s1. Measurement error matters, but even the finite 0.1 scenario remains below the gate.",
    "finite_ceiling_limit": "known_s0p1 is a finite tenfold standard-deviation reduction, not a zero-noise theorem and not a proof that radial geometry alone imposes an absolute ceiling.",
    "resolution_implication": "Information decreases rather than improves toward the higher audited k bins, and no scenario passes any strict bin. Increasing numerical resolution or constructing a high-resolution IC cannot repair missing observational information by itself.",
    "route_implication": "The next scientifically justified route is to augment the z=0 density posterior with independent density-tracer information before IC inversion or nonlinear forward validation."
